<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Osman Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Osman Ali Khan was the seventh and last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, ruling from 1911 to 1948. Reputed the richest man in the world, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmania-university">Osmania University</a> in 1918 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> as its medium of instruction and funded railways, hospitals and public buildings. He sought to keep Hyderabad independent in 1947, but Indian forces annexed the state in September 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Mir Osman Ali Khan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bourne &amp; Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usman_Ali_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mir Osman Ali Khan was born on 5 or 6 April 1886 at Purani Haveli, the second son of Mahboob Ali Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahbub-ali-khan">Asaf Jah VI</a>, and Amat-uz-Zahra Begum. He was educated privately and reportedly became fluent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Persian, Arabic and English; from early 1899 Brian Egerton served for two years as his English tutor. His father died on 29 August 1911, and he was proclaimed Nizam the same day; the coronation was held at the Chowmahalla Palace on 18 September 1911. He lived at King Kothi Palace from the age of 13 until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> was the largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and its ruler was one of only five princes entitled to a 21-gun salute. For his financial contribution to the British war effort in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, amounting to nearly £25 million, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> raised his style in 1918 to His Exalted Highness, and he was named Faithful Ally of the British Government. After the Great Musi Flood of 1908 he had two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-sagar">Osman Sagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himayat-sagar">Himayat Sagar</a>, built on the advice of Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-visvesvaraya">M. Visvesvaraya</a>. In 1919 he formed an Executive Council, and in 1941 the Hyderabad State Bank, which managed the state's own currency.</p><p>When the British withdrew in 1947 the Nizam ruled more than 16 million people. He refused to sign the instrument of accession, preferring a separate kingdom within the British Commonwealth, and signed a one-year standstill agreement instead. He negotiated openly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and covertly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, while his power was weakened by the Telangana Rebellion and by the Razakars, a Muslim militia he could not put down. In 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> invaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a>, under Major General Jayanto Nath Chaudhuri, and the annexation was over in 109 hours. The Nizam kept his personal wealth and title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> of Hyderabad State from 26 January 1950 to 31 October 1956, after which the state was partitioned. He gave 14,000 acres of his estate to the Bhoodan movement of Vinoba Bhave for distribution among landless farmers. He died on 24 February 1967 and was buried the next day, as his will asked, in the Masjid-e Judi, where his mother lay; the chief curator of the Nizam's Museum, D. Bhaskara Rao, put the funeral procession at an estimated one million people. His grandson Mukarram Jah succeeded him as titular Nizam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mir Osman Ali Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mir Mahbub Ali Khan (r. 1869–1911) and <b>Mir Osman Ali Khan</b> (r. 1911–1948) presided over a modernising bureaucracy, though political participation remained limited: an Executive Council was created in 1919, but legislative bodies had only advisory or restricted powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1938" aria-label="Hyderabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6682 1519 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mir Osman Ali Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7132 1789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7132 1789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1938">What was here in 1938? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
